Treating transient caches as authoritative stores results in data loss during restart, memory exhaustion, or network partitioning. Conversely, failing to recognize that a cache is a derived projection leads engineers to deploy ad-hoc invalidations that leave orphaned, indefinitely corrupted state in memory.   

Every cached entity within an enterprise distributed architecture must be governed by an explicit architectural contract answering eight core questions:

Where is the authoritative source? The exact relational database table, document store, or append-only event log that serves as the immutable root of record.   

Why is this data cached? The specific performance or capacity constraint being resolved (e.g., avoiding expensive multi-table relational joins, offloading CPU-intensive JSON serialization, or shielding disk I/O).   

Who populates it? The precise execution path responsible for writing the cache entry (e.g., synchronous read-miss handler, an asynchronous message consumer, or an offline scheduled worker).

Who invalidates it? The exact mutation hook, Change Data Capture (CDC) pipeline, or domain event consumer that signals eviction or mutation.   

How stale may it become? The maximum temporal staleness (Δt 
