Certain categories of system state must never be stored in shared or edge caches. Non-idempotent one-time payment authorization tokens, raw cryptographic secrets, unhashed personal identification credentials, and transient transaction state machines undergoing mutation must be routed directly to authoritative ACID datastores. Caching authorization tokens or user balance structures in public edge caches or shared multi-tenant keys creates risks of session cross-contamination, replay attacks, and state divergence.   

Multi-Level Caching Architecture
When distributed systems must sustain hundreds of thousands of read requests per second, querying a distributed L2 cache over the network introduces connection saturation, serialization overhead, and network interface card (NIC) bottlenecks. Multi-level caching addresses this by placing an in-process, non-blocking L1 cache in application heap memory, backed by a remote distributed L2 cluster, which is backed by the authoritative relational datastore.   

The multi-level read flow traverses the stack strictly from lowest latency to highest latency:

The application thread intercepts the read command and queries the in-process L1 cache (e.g., Caffeine in Java, Moka in Rust, or an in-memory sync structure in Go). If an entry exists and its local lifespan has not expired, the deserialized in-memory pointer is returned immediately, completing the read in tens of nanoseconds.   

On an L1 cache miss, the application issues an asynchronous or pipelined query across the network to the distributed L2 cache (e.g., Redis Cluster). If an entry is present, the byte array is retrieved, deserialized into a domain object, populated into the local L1 cache with an ephemeral TTL (T 
