To enforce bounded coherence across horizontal instances without relying on short TTL polling, systems must implement an active L1 invalidation bus using publish/subscribe infrastructure (such as Redis Pub/Sub, Kafka, or RabbitMQ) or native Redis Client-Side Caching (Tracking API).   

When an application instance writes a mutation to the database and invalidates the L2 key, it broadcasts an invalidation frame containing the key identifier to the invalidation channel. All peer nodes listening on the channel evict the designated key from their local L1 heap, preventing prolonged staleness across horizontal application tiers.   

Multi-level caching is architecturally justified only when profiling demonstrates that network transport to L2, JSON/Protobuf deserialization CPU overhead, or distributed cache hot-keys restrict system scalability. For standard read/write workloads where L2 read latency (< 2 ms) satisfies the SLA, multi-level architectures add operational complexity and debugging overhead that may not be offset by the performance gains.   

