Cache Stampede Mitigation
When a hot key expires, thousands of concurrent threads can simultaneously discover a cache miss, attempting to recompute the data and query the database at the same time.   

Systems use two primary engineering patterns to prevent stampedes:

Single-Flight / Request Coalescing: The application groups in-flight requests by cache key. The first worker thread to encounter a miss initiates the recomputation. Concurrent threads requesting the same key register as passive listeners, waiting on a synchronization construct (e.g., Go singleflight or Java CompletableFuture). When the loader finishes, the resulting payload is broadcast to all waiting callers, resulting in a single database query.   

Probabilistic Early Expiration (XFetch Algorithm): Rather than waiting for a rigid TTL boundary to expire, reading clients execute a probabilistic evaluation on each cache hit. As the remaining TTL diminishes, the probability that a reading worker refreshes the cache entry in the background increases.   

