Production Case Studies and Incident Post-Mortems
Facebook (September 2010): Configuration Stampede and Cascading Collapse
Facebook experienced a multi-hour global outage triggered by a failure in their caching and configuration architecture.   

An engineer pushed a configuration change to fix an internal system. The configuration value was stored in a shared cache cluster and read by application servers globally. Due to an edge-case validation error, the application tier classified the newly fetched configuration value as corrupted.   

The application logic was designed to automatically repair corrupted cache entries by deleting the invalid key and querying the primary MySQL database cluster to recompute and write back the authoritative configuration value.   

Because thousands of application servers globally detected the invalid value simultaneously, they all issued concurrent write and read requests directly to the database tier.   

This thundering herd overwhelmed the MySQL databases, causing query queues to spike and database response times to exceed client timeouts.   

The database timeout exceptions were in turn interpreted as cache read failures, causing the application servers to retry the repair cycle aggressively.   

This established a positive feedback loop (metastable failure state) where the system continued to hammer the saturated databases even after the original invalid configuration value was reverted.   

To restore service, Facebook engineers had to take down all incoming web traffic, stop the application servers, allow the database queues to drain, re-warm the configuration caches offline, and gradually route production traffic back to the platform.   

GitHub (August 2020): Topology Transitions, Capacity Saturation, and Retry Loops
GitHub experienced an outage lasting nearly 8 hours that disrupted core services, pull requests, APIs, and authentication.   

Traffic levels reached an all-time peak, coinciding with infrastructure maintenance in their Central US data center. A key internal database component reached a capacity ceiling under the traffic load. The resulting latency degradation propagated up the stack, causing downstream timeouts in dependent authentication and authorization services.   

Upstream services and client applications did not enforce strict retry budgets or circuit breakers. When requests timed out, aggressive client-side retry loops generated a surge of secondary traffic that amplified the load on the already struggling infrastructure.   

Attempts to bring services back online repeatedly failed because recovering instances were immediately saturated by the accumulated backlog of retries.   

Engineers mitigated the incident by rerouting regional traffic, isolating affected data center components, and rate-limiting incoming client traffic to allow systems to stabilize.   

Following the outage, GitHub updated its engineering standards to mandate retry budgets, adaptive variable timeouts, and stricter isolation boundaries across internal service interactions to prevent retry storms.   

E-Commerce Incident: The Black Friday Cache Penetration Collapse
During a major Black Friday promotional event, an enterprise retail e-commerce platform suffered a database outage driven by un-cached discontinued inventory.   

Traffic spiked to over 100,000 requests per second across product detail pages. External search engines, marketing emails, and scraping bots generated thousands of requests for out-of-stock and discontinued items from previous years.   

The platform implemented standard Cache-Aside caching, but lacked negative caching. When a request arrived for a discontinued product ID, the application missed the cache and queried the relational database.   

The database returned an empty result set (404 Not Found), which the application returned to the client without populating the cache.   

As a result, every subsequent request for a discontinued product bypassed the cache entirely, sending over 10,000 queries per second directly to the database tier.   

The database reached 100% CPU utilization within minutes, connection pools filled, and the platform became unresponsive.   

Engineers recovered the platform by deploying an emergency hotfix that cached negative lookup results (NULL objects) with a 60-second TTL and added request coalescing (single-flight) to consolidate duplicate misses. The fix stabilized the database, reducing miss-driven database traffic by 95%.   

Architectural Anti-Patterns
Caching Without a TTL: Storing keys indefinitely under the assumption that explicit invalidation will catch all mutations. When an unhandled code path or dropped event skips invalidation, the key becomes permanently stale.   

Shared Cache Keys Across Services: Allowing multiple microservices to read or write the same cache keys. This couples deployment lifecycles, makes schema changes brittle, and can cause cross-service deserialization failures.   

Cache as an Accidental Source of Truth: Storing operational or business state exclusively in the cache without writing it to a durable database. If the cache restarts or evicts keys under memory pressure, unrecoverable data loss occurs.   

Unbounded Local In-Memory Maps: Using unmanaged language collections (e.g., standard HashMaps) as in-process caches without size limits or eviction policies, inevitably causing Out-Of-Memory (OOM) crashes.

Bypassing Cache Directly to Database on Outage: Configuring applications to fall back to the database on cache errors without applying concurrency limits or circuit breakers. This often causes the database to collapse under the full, unshielded read load.   

Scattering Cache Logic Across Business Code: Embedding direct Redis calls across controllers and service classes instead of encapsulating access behind a unified data access layer. This complicates testing, obfuscates failure handling, and makes caching policies difficult to maintain.

